const articleHtml = "<p>Racing pigeon lofts run on timing: pairing, weaning, basketing, and race days all happen on a schedule. A structured health screening plan brings the same discipline to disease risk. It answers three questions before problems spread through the loft: which pathogens matter for this flock, which birds and events should trigger testing, and how each result should be handled. This guide explains how to build that plan — including what a pigeon disease testing panel is, when individual pathogen tests make more sense, and why no single list of tests fits every loft. Screening is a risk-management input that supports veterinary interpretation; it is not a diagnosis by itself.</p>\n<h2>Why Racing Pigeons Need Health Screening</h2>\n<p>Racing pigeons are among the most intensively mixed birds in aviculture. Birds from different lofts share transport crates every week, one-loft races pool hundreds of pigeons from different regions, and sales, shows, and new acquisitions keep moving birds between flocks. Each mixing event is a potential exposure event. Several significant pathogens — pigeon paramyxovirus type 1 (PPMV-1), pigeon herpesvirus (PiHV), <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>, and <em>Salmonella</em> — can circulate through carriers that look completely normal. By the time clinical signs appear, the organism may already have moved through a large part of the loft.</p>\n<p>Screening changes that dynamic. Testing new birds before they enter the main loft, testing the racing team before the season, and testing breeders before pairing give a loft manager information while there is still time to act on it. The World Organisation for Animal Health (WOAH) emphasizes that Newcastle disease-type infections, which include PPMV-1, can look like other avian diseases and that laboratory testing is important to confirm what is circulating. For a loft, the practical value is the same: molecular detection tells you whether target nucleic acid is present in submitted samples, so quarantine, vaccination, and management decisions can rest on data rather than guesswork.</p>\n<p>That said, screening is not a surveillance program you run for its own sake. Each test should be tied to a decision — admit or reject, race or hold back, pair or postpone — and results should always be read together with clinical observations and veterinary advice.</p>\n<h2>Common Disease Risks in Racing Pigeon Lofts</h2>\n<p>Pigeon disease risk is easiest to understand by how pathogens enter and move through a loft.</p>\n<p><strong>Mixing and transport.</strong> Baskets, transporters, show cages, and shared drinkers are the classic routes for PPMV-1, PiHV, and <em>Trichomonas gallinae</em>. Birds that never leave the loft still share air, feed, and equipment with birds that do.</p>\n<p><strong>Stress-triggered flare-ups.</strong> Young bird season, the molt, overcrowding, heat, and repeated basketing suppress immunity and turn subclinical infections into visible problems. This is why many lofts see enteric and respiratory issues cluster around the same calendar windows each year.</p>\n<p><strong>Different presentations point in different directions.</strong> Respiratory and diphtheritic signs suggest PiHV or chlamydiosis; neurological signs raise PPMV-1; watery droppings in young birds bring pigeon adenovirus (PiADV), pigeon rotavirus (PiRV), and <em>Salmonella</em> into the differential; oral and crop lesions point to trichomoniasis.</p>\n<p><strong>Young pigeon disease syndrome is multifactorial.</strong> Pigeon circovirus (PiCV) is frequently detected in affected young pigeons, but the syndrome is generally understood as an interaction of viruses, secondary infections, and management factors rather than a single-agent disease. A screening plan should treat it the same way — several targets, not one.</p>\n<p><strong>Some pathogens are zoonotic.</strong> <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em> can cause psittacosis in people, and <em>Salmonella</em> can also infect humans, so testing has value beyond the birds themselves. The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) notes that people exposed to infected birds or dried droppings are at risk, which makes molecular screening a sensible precaution for lofts with staff or frequent visitors.</p>\n<h2>Key Pathogens to Consider in Pigeon Screening</h2>\n<p>No universal panel fits every loft. The right list depends on the region, the loft's history, the birds' contact network, and what a veterinarian is trying to rule in or out. The pathogens below are the ones most often discussed when racing pigeon screening plans are put together.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pathogen (category)</th>\n<th>Why it matters in a loft</th>\n<th>Typical sample considerations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pigeon circovirus, PiCV (virus)</td>\n<td>Associated with young pigeon disease syndrome; immunosuppression concern in young birds</td>\n<td>Feather pulp, blood, or cloacal material</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pigeon adenovirus, PiADV (virus)</td>\n<td>Enteric and hepatic disease in young birds (type I) and older birds (type II)</td>\n<td>Cloacal swab, feces, or liver tissue at necropsy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pigeon paramyxovirus type 1, PPMV-1 (virus)</td>\n<td>Neurological and enteric signs; WOAH-listed disease; core of many vaccination programs</td>\n<td>Cloacal and oropharyngeal swabs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pigeon herpesvirus, PiHV (virus)</td>\n<td>Respiratory and diphtheritic signs; lifelong carriers; transmitted to squabs in crop milk</td>\n<td>Oropharyngeal or conjunctival swabs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pigeon rotavirus, PiRV (virus)</td>\n<td>Enteric signs, mainly in young pigeons; also detected in birds without diarrhea</td>\n<td>Cloacal swab or feces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Chlamydia psittaci</em> (bacterium)</td>\n<td>Respiratory and systemic disease; intermittent shedding; zoonotic</td>\n<td>Combined cloacal and choanal swabs, feces, or blood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Salmonella</em> spp. (bacterium)</td>\n<td>Enteric disease, joint and neurologic involvement; persistent carriers; zoonotic</td>\n<td>Feces, cloacal swab, or organs at necropsy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Trichomonas gallinae</em> (protozoan)</td>\n<td>Canker; passed from parents to squabs in crop milk</td>\n<td>Crop or oropharyngeal swab</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This table is a starting framework, not a prescription. Regional prevalence, loft history, and a veterinarian's differential list should decide which targets actually get ordered — and the testing sequence used throughout this guide is: species → exposure risk → target pathogens → sample type → testing method.</p>\n<h2>What Is a Pigeon Disease Testing Panel?</h2>\n<p>A pigeon disease testing panel is a defined set of molecular assays run together on shared sample material. In practice, a laboratory extracts nucleic acid from submitted swabs, feathers, or tissue and then runs individual real-time PCR (qPCR) assays for each target in the panel. The report usually states a result per pathogen — for example, Detected, Not Detected, Invalid, or Inconclusive — rather than a single overall score.</p>\n<p>Three points matter when choosing between a panel and individual pathogen tests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A panel conserves samples and time.</strong> One submission can answer several questions, which is valuable when sample volume is limited or when a new bird needs a fast quarantine decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Every target still has to stand on its own.</strong> Multiplex and multi-target approaches only help if each assay has been validated for the relevant sample type, with defined detection limits and documented freedom from cross-reactivity. Veterinary laboratory accreditation frameworks, such as those of the American Association of Veterinary Laboratory Diagnosticians (AAVLD), treat per-assay validation and quality control as core requirements — a good reason to ask any laboratory for validation data per target rather than for the panel as a vague whole.</li>\n<li><strong>Panel size is not a quality metric.</strong> A longer list of targets is not automatically better screening. The practical conclusion is that the decision should follow the loft's situation, not a one-size-fits-all count. A panel that includes irrelevant targets adds cost and interpretive noise without adding safety.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Molecular panels also answer a different question than serology. PCR-based tests detect pathogen nucleic acid in the submitted sample at the time of collection, which reflects current presence and shedding; antibody tests reflect exposure and immune history. The two are complementary — neither replaces the other.</p>\n<p>Finally, keep result limits in mind. A Not Detected result means the target nucleic acid was not found in that sample at that moment — it does not prove the bird is free of the pathogen, because shedding can be intermittent, especially with <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>. A Detected result means the target was found, not that the bird is clinically ill or that every detected pathogen explains the symptoms. Cycle threshold (Ct) values can help a laboratory describe relative target quantity, but they are not a severity score.</p>\n<h2>Matching Screening to Loft Situations</h2>\n<p>The most useful screening is tied to a concrete management event. The matrix below follows the decision directions recommended for racing lofts; in every row, the final selection should be narrowed with a veterinarian.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Testing considerations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New pigeon entering the loft</td>\n<td>Source history, prior exposure, and the current epidemiology of the receiving loft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Before racing season</td>\n<td>Competition region, previous years' disease records, and flock management risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neurological signs in the loft</td>\n<td>PPMV-1 and related targets; the veterinarian decides further workup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Young birds with enteric signs</td>\n<td>PiADV, PiRV, bacterial agents, and other differentials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long-term breeding management</td>\n<td>PiCV, PiADV, and other targets relevant to flock health</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After transport or racing events</td>\n<td>Contact risk and local or regional prevalence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Screening before racing season</h3>\n<p>Pre-season screening is about protecting the whole season, not just the first race. Many lofts test the racing team a few weeks before basketing begins so results are back before birds start mixing. Typical targets follow the loft's history: PPMV-1 where vaccination programs need verification, PiHV and chlamydia in lofts with respiratory history, and trichomoniasis checks before birds share drinkers on transporters. Pair screening with a veterinary health check — molecular results cannot replace a clinical examination.</p>\n<h3>Testing newly acquired racing pigeons</h3>\n<p>New birds are the single biggest controlled risk in a loft. A structured approach is to test during quarantine rather than before it: collect samples shortly after arrival, hold the bird in isolation while results come back, and only then decide on entry into the main loft. Which targets depend on the seller, the bird's travel history, and what is known to circulate in the receiving loft. A negative screening result supports the quarantine decision but does not replace the quarantine period itself, because no single sampling moment can rule out every pathogen.</p>\n<h3>Screening during breeding season</h3>\n<p>Breeding birds should be screened before pairing, not after eggs are laid, because several pathogens move from parents to squabs. PiHV and <em>Trichomonas gallinae</em> pass in crop milk, and PiCV and PiADV matter for young bird health. Screening breeders ahead of pairing lets a loft separate or treat before the breeding cycle starts, and it gives the veterinarian a baseline if young bird problems appear later.</p>\n<h3>What to do after a positive result</h3>\n<p>A Detected result is the start of a decision, not the end of one. The practical sequence is: isolate the bird and review the report with a veterinarian; compare the finding with clinical signs, loft history, and the other birds' results; confirm anything unexpected with retesting or complementary methods before acting; and record the outcome in the loft's health log. Retest timing depends on the pathogen — intermittent shedders may need repeated sampling before a bird can be declared low-risk. For reportable situations such as PPMV-1, follow national or regional reporting requirements and notify the relevant race or club authorities where applicable. Above all, do not treat blindly on a molecular result alone; treatment choices belong with the veterinarian.</p>\n<h2>Scenario: Twelve yearlings arrive ten days before basketing</h2>\n<p>A racing loft buys 12 yearlings from three lofts ten days before basketing. The birds look normal, but one group travelled together, another came through a dealer, and the third arrived directly from a breeder. The loft can isolate the 12 and delay their first basketing. The uncertainty is whether recent transport and mixing introduced an infection that is not yet visible, and which targets deserve priority.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hold all 12 away from the racing team and record source loft, transport, and shared crates. Do not let race entry dictate sampling.</li>\n<li>Have a veterinarian prioritize PPMV-1, pigeon herpesvirus, <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>, and <em>Salmonella</em>; consider <em>Trichomonas</em> or circovirus only when exposure or signs support them.</li>\n<li>Submit voluntary screening while the birds remain separate. Check whether any finding triggers regulatory reporting, which is separate. See our <a href=\"/multiplex-pcr-avian-disease-testing/\">multiplex PCR guide</a> when comparing targets and validation.</li>\n<li>If Not Detected, keep observing and ask whether basketing leaves room for a repeat. A negative result lowers risk but does not clear the loft.</li>\n<li>If Detected or Inconclusive, hold the group, isolate affected birds, trace contacts, and involve the veterinarian. Culling or treatment is not decided from one result.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Result</th><th>What changes</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Not Detected</td><td>Continue observation; decide whether timing allows a repeat before basketing.</td><td>The 12 are free of infection or the loft is clear.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Detected</td><td>Isolate the relevant birds, trace transport and loft contacts, and obtain veterinary review.</td><td>Which birds were exposed, when infection occurred, or that the whole loft is affected.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inconclusive or Invalid</td><td>Do not release the group from separation; arrange a new sample or method as advised.</td><td>Presence or absence of the target.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What we would do:</strong> We would test the 12 before basketing and keep race plans provisional, because the next ten days contain both a new mixing event and a scheduled contact event. The wider <a href=\"/bird-health-screening-biosecurity/\">loft screening and biosecurity plan</a> should set isolation and repeat timing, not the calendar alone. This advice would not apply if a bird showed neurological or respiratory signs or a reportable disease were suspected; urgent veterinary and official action would come first.</p>\n<p>This is a testing-strategy example, not a diagnosis or treatment plan, and a veterinarian must interpret results with clinical findings.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Pigeon Disease Screening</h2>\n<h3>Do all racing pigeons need the same disease testing panel?</h3>\n<p>No. Testing selection should follow the bird's species, exposure history, the events the loft is preparing for, and the clinical picture — not a fixed list applied to every flock. A quarantine intake panel for a newly purchased bird may legitimately differ from a pre-breeding panel for an established breeder.</p>\n<h3>Does a negative PCR result mean my pigeon is free of disease?</h3>\n<p>No. A Not Detected result means the target nucleic acid was not found in that sample at that time. Shedding can be intermittent — <em>Chlamydia psittaci</em> is a well-known example — and sampling site, timing, and transport all affect detection. Negative results reduce risk; they do not eliminate it.</p>\n<h3>Does a positive result mean my pigeon is sick?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily. A Detected result shows that target nucleic acid was present in the submitted sample. Birds can carry and shed pathogens while showing no clinical signs. Whether a finding explains illness requires veterinary interpretation together with the clinical picture.</p>\n<h3>Which samples should I submit for pigeon screening?</h3>\n<p>It depends on the target pathogen. Combined cloacal and oropharyngeal swabs cover many viruses and bacteria, feather pulp is used for circovirus-type targets, and crop swabs are used for <em>Trichomonas gallinae</em>. Always follow the laboratory's collection instructions for the specific panel ordered.</p>\n<h3>Can one set of samples be tested for several pathogens at once?</h3>\n<p>Yes — many pigeon disease testing panels run several PCR assays from shared extracted nucleic acid, which saves sample volume and time. The important check is whether each individual target has been validated for the sample type you submitted, rather than assuming one panel guarantee covers all targets equally.</p>\n<h3>How often should a racing loft screen?</h3>\n<p>There is no universal schedule. Tie testing to events: new arrivals, pre-season preparation, breeding, transport-heavy periods, and any suspected exposure. Screening frequency is a management decision, and no testing program replaces day-to-day biosecurity such as isolation, cleaning, and controlled access, as emphasized in USDA APHIS biosecurity guidance.</p>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO, the avian molecular testing laboratory behind AviantestPro, provides qPCR-based pigeon pathogen detection and screening panel services, including the tests referenced throughout this guide. We want to be clear about what this article is and is not: this is a testing-strategy judgment based on published veterinary guidance, not a veterinary diagnosis, and no statement here should be read as diagnostic or treatment advice. Molecular screening results describe whether target nucleic acid was detected in submitted samples; they are not standalone disease diagnoses and should always be interpreted by a veterinarian together with clinical findings.</p>\n<p>When choosing any laboratory, ask the same questions you would ask of a veterinary partner: which targets are validated for which sample types, what the detection limits are, how results are reported (Detected, Not Detected, Invalid, Inconclusive), how inconclusive samples are re-run, and what the turnaround time is for quarantine decisions. Laboratories operating under AAVLD-style quality frameworks make this information available. Screening works best as one layer of a biosecurity program — quarantine, hygiene, records, and veterinary review do the rest.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>World Organisation for Animal Health (WOAH) — Newcastle disease: https://www.woah.org/en/disease/newcastle-disease/</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Trichomonosis in Birds: https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/trichomonosis/trichomonosis-in-birds</li>\n<li>American Association of Veterinary Laboratory Diagnosticians (AAVLD) — Accreditation Requirements: https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n<li>U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) — About Psittacosis: https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock biosecurity program: https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>Łukaszuk E, Dziewulska D, Stenzel T. Rotaviruses in Pigeons With Diarrhea: Recovery of Three Complete Pigeon Rotavirus A Genomes and the First Case of Pigeon Rotavirus G in Europe. Transbound Emerg Dis. 2024. doi:10.1155/tbed/4684235 — https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40303061/</li>\n</ol>";
